  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nCurrently, S/WV mid level ridge building into the Northern\nPlains, with weak/subtle surface high pressure creeping across\nthe Dakotas. Sunny west with low clouds dissipating over my east\nearly this afternoon, leads to temperatures in the lower 80s\nsouthwest and low 70s elsewhere west and central. Cooler\nunderneath the stratus. \n\nModels remain in good agreement with fog redeveloping east to\nwest tonight through Friday morning, with lingering boundary\nlayer moisture, subsidence, and light and variable/light\nsoutherly winds. RAP/HRRR start out the fog in my southeast\n04-06Z, then expanding west into central areas of the state\novernight, possibly for a decent portion of the southwest as\nwell. Will continue to mention in the HWO. \n\nIncreasing return flow on Friday as lee side low pressure \ndevelops in response to a southeast to northwest orientated \nupper level trough moving across the Rockies. Breezy weather\nresults on Friday, with temperatures cooling slightly compared\nto Thu. The aforementioned upper trough swings through the \nDakotas Fri/Fri night, with the better chances for precipitation\nalong to east of an associated sfc trough positioned over \ncentral ND. \n\nWe remain in a strong southwest flow aloft pattern through the\nweekend into early next week, with models in great agreement\nbringing a well defined lead embedded wave across the region \nSunday night into Monday, coupled with a favorable upper jet \ndynamics and a well defined frontal boundary. Strong 0-6 km wind\nshear with up to around 1000 J/KG of MUCAPE will support \nscattered thunderstorms as well, a few on the stronger side \n(machine learning suggest severe weather possible). An active\nsouthwest flow remains for Monday, with Sunday's sfc trough\nstalling from central SD into portions of central and eastern\nNorth Dakota. Instability and shear remain present for another\npotential round of strong convection Mon aft/eve along and east\nof the sfc boundary, with mainly dry conditions favored west.  \n\nQuasi-zonal flow pattern after next week Monday, with near to\nslightly below seasonal temperatures and minimal chances for\nprecipitation.",
